Q: Hide text, leave icon elements A: <p>Some additional notes on the previous approaches:</p>
<h3>Hide via <code>font-size</code></h3>
<p>As suggested by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/34000189/15015675">Rokin</a> you can easily override a child elements font-size in a parent element with <code>font-size:0</code>.</p>
<p>However, you may run into troubles because most icon libraries depend on CSS stylesheets mapping icon class names to <code>:before</code> unicode points and applying basic font-size inheritance.</p>
<p>In the below snippet the icons also inherit <code>font-size:0</code> due to default fontAwesome style rules (abbreviated):</p>
<pre><code>.fa {
    display: inline-block;
    line-height: 1;
    font-family: FontAwesome;
    font-size: inherit;
}

.fa-home:before {
  content: &quot;\f015&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding:0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
}



@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* hide button text */
  .btn {
    font-size: 0;
  }

  /* override font-size */
  .fa {
    font-size: initial;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><strong>Workaround</strong> just add another rule with higher <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascade/Specificity" rel="nofollow noreferrer">specificity</a> like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* 2 selectors - higher specificity */
.btn .fa {
  font-size: 1rem;
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding:0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
}


/* override font-size */
.btn .fa {
  font-size: 1rem;
}

@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* hide button text */
  .btn {
    font-size: 0;
  }

}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>However, this introduces another problem: we can't use relative font-sizes inherited from the parent element. Instead we need to use more explicit size values such as <code>rem</code> (relative to HTML root) or <code>px</code>.</p>
<h3>Hide via »cropping«</h3>
<p>As described by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/34000082/15015675">Mr Lister</a> we may simply crop the button element itself to a specific rectangle.</p>
<p>Since most icon libraries are designed using a square reference frame, we can simply set dimension so e.g <code>width:1em; height:1em</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
  overflow: hidden;
}


@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .btn {
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding: 0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
  overflow: hidden;
}



@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* crop button text */
  .btn {
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
  }

}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h3>Hiding icons injected as SVGs</h3>
<p>Modern flavors of icon libraries such as FontAwesome, MaterialIcons etc. often prefer script based <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> injection over font specific embedding concepts.</p>
<p>Nonetheless these usually also make an SVG icon behave like an inline element so you may also use the font-size approach as long as you also target the injected SVG class.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* override svg font-size */
.btn svg {
  font-size: 1rem;
  height: 1em;
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding: 0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.btn2 {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
}

/* override svg font-size */
.btn2 svg {
  font-size: 1rem;
  height: 1em;
}

@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* crop button text */
  .btn {
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
  }

  /* hide button text via font-size */
  .btn2 {
    font-size: 0;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://use.fontawesome.com/releases/v6.3.0/js/all.js" crossorigin="anonymous"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Resize me&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;

  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;a href="" class="btn"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href="" class="btn"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;

  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;a href="" class="btn2"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href="" class="btn2"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h3>Font based icons and accessibility</h3>
<p>When you're using icons based on pseudo element text content such as FontAwesome legacy API it is highly recommended to attribute these as rather decorative to prevent assistive technologies such as screen readers from reading rather gibberish PUA unicode text as used by FA.</p>
<p>For instance you can add a <code>aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot;</code> attribute like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;a href=&quot;&quot; class=&quot;btn&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa fa-arrow-right&quot; aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
Button 1
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>See also the <a href="https://fontawesome.com/v4/accessibility" rel="nofollow noreferrer">fontawesome guide</a></p>
<p>Aforementioned font-size approach may also be overridden by some mobile browsers as these often implemented some default minimal font-sizes for the sake of readability. You can usually override these as well. However, the »cropping« concept might be more robust in this regard.</p>

Q: Best way to iterate in a triangular fashion over a self-crossproduct of a container A: <p>First upgrade: from C++11 on, you can use <strong>range based <code>for</code></strong>(<em>foreach</em>) for cleaner syntax, and bounds safety:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>for(auto&amp; d1 : my_data)
    for(auto&amp; d2: my_data)
       use(d1, d2);
</code></pre>
<p>The iterators are basically programmer invisible, reducing probability of common pitfalls.</p>
<p>Combining C++20 <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/header/ranges" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code></a> with <strong>structured binding</strong> from C++17 allows for simpler expresion of Cartesian product:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ranges&gt;
for( auto [d1, d2, d3] 
   : std::views::cartesian_product
   ( my_data1, my_data1, my_data2 ) )
   use(d1, d2, d3);
</code></pre>
<p>It's possible to use views as multi-references to original containers too:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto cart_prod 
= std::views::cartesian_product
  ( my_data, my_data );

for(auto [d1, d2]: cart_prod)
    use(d1, d2);

</code></pre>
<p>The specific case of C++23 <code>cartesian_product</code> is unfortunately suboptimal in terms of performance. Compared to that, the rest of <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code> is a great tool for clean and efficient development. However The performance deficiency of <code>cartesian_product</code> seems to have slowed down adoption of other <em>triangular views</em> that you mentioned. It's possible to define limited versions of these views in own library using C++23 <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/ranges/view_interface" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges::view_interface</code></a> and <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/ranges/range_adaptor_closure" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges::range_adaptor_closure</code></a>.</p>
<p>For now, the simplest possible form of upper triangular iteration is nested <code>for</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto b_data
= orig_b_data
| std::views::all;

auto ib = b_data.begin();
for( auto&amp; a : a_data )
    for( auto&amp; b
       : std::views::subrange
         (ib++, b_data.end())
       ) uptriangle(a,b);
</code></pre>
<p>To drop diagonal, just change the initialization:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto b_data
= orig_b_data
| std::views::drop(1);
</code></pre>
<p>In order to make bottom triangle change the <code>subrange</code> bounds:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::views::subrange
( b_data.begin(), ib++ )
</code></pre>
<p>Now if the initialization takes <code>all</code>, the digonal is dropped in the bottom triangle; otherwise if the <code>drop(1)</code> is used, the diagonal is included. In short words, changing the <code>subrange</code> bounds as above complements the iteration on top triangle.</p>
<p>In order to avoid UB at the end of increment operation, you can use <code>std::ranged::next</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>{ std::views::subrange
  ( ib, b_data.end() );
  ib =std::ranged::next
  (ib, b_data.end() ); };
</code></pre>
<p>This might look too verbose but the end iterator is always a pain the neck. I hope the implementation of <code>cartesian_product</code> gets optimized, so the triangular views get a chance for standardization, simplifying the composition.</p>

Q: VS2012 Code Analysis Warning C6011 not showing all potential dereferencing of null pointers A: <p>Your example continues after printing the error, and then dereferences the NULL pointer.  The tool correctly detects this mistake but may not explain it to you.</p>
<p>If your code replaced the printf() with a custom printf_and_abort() function with appropriate declaration, the dereferencing will not happen and the tool should be happy.</p>
<pre><code>/* Implementing this or anything similar is left as an exercise for the reader or their
 *    software library provider.
 */
int __declspec(noreturn) printf_and_abort(const char *fmt, ...);

struct test_struct
{
    unsigned int    int_test;
};

void set_to_1000(struct test_struct* const test)
{
    if (test == NULL) {
        printf_and_abort(&quot;Error in set_to_1000, test is NULL)\n&quot;);
    }
    test-&gt;int_test = 1000;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Generating grouped PDF from paginated API data in React/jsPDF without loading all rows into memory A: <p>The memory constraint you can fix and the one you can't are different, and separating them helps.</p>
<p><strong>jsPDF holds the whole document, not just the whole dataset.</strong> You can avoid keeping 50k rows of JSON around: fetch a page, feed it to autoTable, drop it, keep only running totals. But the <code>jsPDF</code> instance grows with every row you draw and <code>jspdf-autotable</code> keeps its parsed row model per table. At 50k rows you are building a several-hundred-page document, so the doc object becomes the ceiling and streaming the input does not remove it. jsPDF is also synchronous, so the tab is frozen for the whole build and mobile Safari will probably kill it first.</p>
<p><strong>The prerequisite that decides whether this works at all: the API has to be sorted.</strong> Page-boundary grouping only works if rows arrive ordered by <code>AccountHead</code>, then <code>AccountNumber</code>. If the endpoint does not guarantee that, a group can reappear three pages later, you can never safely close a subtotal when the key changes, and you are forced to buffer everything anyway. Also move off <code>OFFSET</code> pagination to keyset/cursor if you have not: at 50k rows OFFSET gets progressively slower and will duplicate or skip rows if anything is inserted mid-export.</p>
<p>Given sorted input, I think the client-side shape is:</p>
<pre><code>const doc = new jsPDF();
let curGroup = null, curAccount = null;
let acctTotal = 0, groupTotal = 0, grandTotal = 0;
let cursor = null, block = [];

const flushAccount = () =&gt; {
  if (curAccount === null) return;
  doc.autoTable({
    startY: doc.lastAutoTable?.finalY ?? 20,
    body: block.concat([[`Account total ${curAccount}`, acctTotal.toFixed(2)]]),
    rowPageBreak: 'avoid',
    didDrawPage: drawReportHeader,
  });
  block = [];
  groupTotal += acctTotal;
  acctTotal = 0;
};

do {
  const { Result, nextCursor } = await fetchPage(cursor);

  for (const row of Result) {
    if (row.AccountNumber !== curAccount) { flushAccount(); curAccount = row.AccountNumber; }
    if (row.GroupName !== curGroup) { /* emit group total, grandTotal += groupTotal, reset */ curGroup = row.GroupName; }
    block.push(toCells(row));
    acctTotal += Number(row.Amount);
  }

  cursor = nextCursor;   // the page just processed is now collectable
} while (cursor);
flushAccount();
</code></pre>
<p>Batch per account block as above rather than calling <code>autoTable</code> once per row, which is very slow. <code>rowPageBreak: 'avoid'</code> stops a subtotal splitting off its block, <code>didDrawPage</code> redraws the column header on each page, and running the loop in a Web Worker keeps the main thread alive.</p>
<p><strong>For 50k rows I would move this server-side though</strong>, because the problem fits there properly:</p>
<p>Let SQL do the totals. <code>ROLLUP</code> gives you account, group and grand totals in one pass, so all the accumulator bookkeeping above disappears:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT AccountHead, AccountNumber, SUM(Amount)
FROM entries
GROUP BY ROLLUP (AccountHead, AccountNumber)
ORDER BY AccountHead, AccountNumber;
</code></pre>
<p>Rows with <code>AccountNumber IS NULL</code> are group totals, both-NULL is the grand total.</p>
<p>Then actually stream. A Node service using <code>pdfkit</code> pipes straight into the HTTP response, so peak memory is about one page instead of one document. jsPDF has no streaming equivalent. The browser just receives a download.</p>
<p>One reframe worth putting to whoever asked for this: a several-hundred-page PDF is rarely what someone wants from a &quot;report&quot;. Check whether they need a summary PDF (rollup levels only, a few pages) plus a streaming XLSX export (<code>exceljs</code> has a streaming writer) for row-level detail. For 50k rows of financial data that is usually both faster and more useful than the PDF.</p>
<p>Disclosure: I build <a href="https://polydoc.tech" rel="nofollow noreferrer">polydoc.tech</a>, an HTML-to-PDF API, so weigh this last paragraph accordingly. If you do go server-side, the other authoring option is to render the report as HTML and convert it: grouped tables with repeating headers and page-break control are declarative in CSS paged media (<code>thead</code> repeats automatically, <code>break-inside: avoid</code> holds a block together) rather than hand-managed <code>startY</code> arithmetic. It does not dodge the size problem, you would still chunk a report this big, but the authoring model fits grouped reports much better than placing cells imperatively.</p>
